<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Partial Info.plist, merged over xtool's generated one (0.7.2, LR1).

  Referenced by `infoPath:` in xtool.yml. xtool 1.17 builds its own plist in
  memory, then shallow-merges this file's top-level keys over it with ours
  winning, so only the keys we actually want to state belong here — this is not
  a full plist and must not become one.

  Four keys are stamped *after* the merge and cannot be overridden from here:
  UIRequiredDeviceCapabilities, LSRequiresIPhoneOS, CFBundleSupportedPlatforms
  and CFBundleIconFile. Everything else is fair game, including the version
  string — see the note in AppVersion.swift, which predates this file.

  The orientation lock is deliberately NOT here. It lives in
  AppDelegate.application(_:supportedInterfaceOrientationsFor:), which is
  consulted per window and takes precedence over the plist regardless; adding a
  second declaration would create two places to keep in agreement and no new
  guarantee. See the note in xtool.yml.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      LABEL SCAN. Says what the camera is for and where the photograph goes,
      because "Vinodex would like to access the camera" is the prompt a user
      declines. The on-device claim is literally true: OCR is Apple Vision
      running locally and nothing is uploaded anywhere.
    -->
    <key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Vinodex uses the camera to read wine labels. Photos are processed on this device and never uploaded.</string>
    <!--
      The library route runs through PhotosPicker, which is out-of-process and
      does not strictly require this string. It is here anyway: the moment
      anything touches PHPhotoLibrary directly the app would crash on launch
      without it, and a usage description that is merely unused costs nothing.
    -->
    <key>NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Vinodex can read a wine label from a photo you choose. Photos are processed on this device and never uploaded.</string>
    <!--
      Export compliance, answered once here instead of per-build in App Store
      Connect. Verified 2026-08-13: no CryptoKit/CommonCrypto/custom crypto
      anywhere in Sources; the app implements no encryption of its own.
    -->
    <key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
    <false/>
</dict>
</plist>
